.TH UUENCODE 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
uuencode \- encode a binary file into printable text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uuencode
.RB [ \-m ]
.RI [ source_file ]
.I decode_pathname
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uuencode
reads
.I source_file
(or, if it is omitted, standard input) and writes an encoded
representation of it to standard output, using only printable ASCII
characters. The encoded text begins with a header line naming
.I decode_pathname
and the file's permission mode, is followed by the encoded data itself
in fixed-size chunks, and ends with a data-end marker followed by a
line reading
.BR end .
.I decode_pathname
is recorded in the header purely as the name
.BR uudecode (1)
should recreate the file under; it is not opened or read by
.B uuencode
itself.
.PP
The permission mode recorded in the header is, when
.I source_file
was given, that file's own real permission bits, written as three
octal digits. When reading standard input instead (no
.I source_file
operand), there is no real file to derive a mode from, so a fixed mode
of 0644 is recorded instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m
Requests Base64 encoding instead of the traditional uuencode algorithm
described above. This is not implemented:
.B \-m
is refused with a diagnostic and a nonzero exit status, rather than
silently falling back to the traditional encoding under a flag that
promised something else.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I source_file
The file to read and encode. If omitted, standard input is read
instead, and the header's recorded permission mode falls back to 0644
as described above.
.TP
.I decode_pathname
Required. The pathname recorded in the header line for
.BR uudecode (1)
to recreate the file as.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was encoded and written successfully.
.TP
.B 1
.I source_file
could not be opened, a read error occurred while encoding,
.I decode_pathname
was not given (or too many operands were given), or
.B \-m
was requested.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uudecode (1)
